  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nIt's a tranquil, seasonably cool Friday afternoon across south \ncentral MN and western WI. 500mb analysis displays troughing \nover the eastern CONUS, which brought a cold Canadian air mass \nsouth across the Great Lakes and into the Ohio Valley. Satellite\ncaptures cloud cover drifting off of several of the Great Lakes\nowing to the cold air mass aloft (it's hard to miss the \nimpressive band of lake effect rain/snow within the long \nnortherly fetch across Lake Michigan!). Locally, we've witnessed\nefficient erosion of stratus downstream of Lake Superior that \nblanketed much of western WI this morning. Satellite shows that \na few diurnal Cu are all that remain, which has given way to \nmostly sunny skies across this portion of the forecast area. \nDrier air west of the MN/WI line has promoted the persistence of\nabundantly sunny skies. Afternoon temperatures are on track to \nfinish a few degrees below normal in the upper 30s/near 40 \ndegrees. Large scale subsidence associated with ~1020s mb \nsurface high will support a clear, calm, and cold night with \nlows forecast to fall into the upper teens to lower 20s (Coolest\noutside of the TC Metro). \n\nQuiet, sunny weather persists this weekend under the influence of \nhigh pressure and the eastward translation of a ridge aloft. \nTemperatures warm into the lower 40s tomorrow afternoon, which is \ncloser to \"normal\" for the middle of November. On Sunday, winds will \nturn southerly on the western extent of the surface high. This will \ncommence a multi-day warming trend that will be reinforced by the \nexpansion of +10-12C 850mb temperature anomalies across the \nUpper Midwest early next week. Latest forecast from the NBM \nfeatures highs in the upper 40s on Sunday, the lower 50s on \nMonday, the mid 50s on Tuesday, and potentially the mid to upper\n50s Wednesday & Thursday. Morning lows will run warmer than \nnormal afternoon highs by the middle of next week, capturing the\nanomalous extent of the upcoming forecast.\n\nThe forecast becomes more active mid-week onward as an active jet \nsends a series of Pacific waves across the CONUS. The first takes \nthe shape of a longwave trough set to come ashore Tuesday near Baja \nCalifornia and lifts northeast across the Plains Wednesday. Most of \nthe moisture associated with this system aims to fall as precipitation\non the cool/northwest side of the low, which will be well to \nthe northwest of our forecast area. NBM features chance PoPs for\nrain showers across eastern MN and western WI within the warm \nadvective wing of the system. Forecast confidence decreases \nbeyond next Thursday due to a spread in the handling of the \nupper-level features. However, there is a growing deterministic,\nensemble, and machine learning trend that supports a larger \nsystem taking residence across the central CONUS towards the \nend of next weekend, which could bring widespread precipitation\nchances into the region. Cooler, but still mild temperatures in\nthe extended period would favor rain as the dominant \nprecipitation type during the aforementioned timeframe.",
